WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.020
This is Gudetama.

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:04.300
It’s an egg yolk with a little butt crack.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.740
Gudetama looks like a character someone gave up on… it has limbs but no fingers or toes.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:13.040
It has a mouth but no teeth — and yet people can’t get enough of it.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.780
You can find it on backpacks, cups, airplanes, credit cards,

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.800
and it even has its own themed cafe.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.860
But Gudetama’s cute looks aren’t the driving force behind its insane popularity.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:27.800
Its main attraction is its lazy personality.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:42.170
Gudetama comes from a Japanese company called Sanrio.

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:43.379
You might have heard of them.

00:00:43.379 --> 00:00:45.670
They’re the creators behind Hello Kitty.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:51.580
In 2013, Sanrio held a company wide competition to come up with a food-based character.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:55.000
And once people voted, Gudetama didn't end up on top.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.190
DAVE MARCHI: Kirimichan, the salmon filet came in first — we actually started to release products

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:03.149
based on the salmon filet and its friends.

00:01:03.149 --> 00:01:09.340
Gudetama, the lazy egg came in second, but we also released products based on Gudetama

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:11.940
and it really really took off.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:20.070
The appeal of Gudetama’s melancholy stands
in contrast to the American concept of cuteness,

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.150
which is pretty straightforward.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:27.950
The idea of cute represents goodness and optimism,
while pessimism tends to define evil.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:30.180
This is evident in some of Disney’s early
films.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:31.840
SNOW WHITE: Oh, they do look very delicious!

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:32.760
WITCH: Yes...

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:36.960
As you can see— there’s a clear divide between good and evil.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.980
Villains are usually depicted as unappealing, scary, and old —

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:43.240
draped in shadows and dark colors.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:47.260
They are meant to be identified as evil, which
means that they can never be cute.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:50.080
But in Japan, there’s more of a gray area
to this.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:55.140
The word "kawaii" is widely used to describe
the quality of being like a child — which

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.870
means that you can be cute and lazy at the same time.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:11.480
The term emerged in the 1970s and became a
big part of Japanese culture.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.590
It was shown through fashion, handwriting,
and even behavior.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:20.200
And many Japanese artists and academics believed
that this popular culture around cuteness

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.760
happened for a reason.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:30.580
In Japan, the kawaii culture is often linked
to the country’s post-WWII years.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:33.120
TRUMAN: ... a message from the Japanese government.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:38.180
I deem this reply a full acceptance of the Potsdam Declaration...

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:45.500
The idea is that because of its trauma and defeat, the country leaned into its vulnerability.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:51.600
And since then, the concept of kawaii has grown
and even formed smaller subgroups.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.740
This is kimo-kawaii which is sometimes also called gro-kawaii.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.520
And there’s Yuru-kawaii.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.380
Yuru means relaxed and calm.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:04.160
According to cartooning expert Aya Kakeda,
this particular group became popular because

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:06.520
of the stress in modern society.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:11.460
She points out that in the US, people are
drawn to spas and meditation for relaxation.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:16.540
But in Japan, Yuru’s calm appearance brings
comfort to a lot of people.

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:20.360
You can also see a shift in Sanrio’s characters
throughout the years.

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:24.080
They’ve started giving them a personality
to make them more relatable.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:29.280
When Hello Kitty came out in 1974 — she was
more traditionally cute than Gudetama

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.740
but she has remained somewhat emotionless.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:34.980
She doesn’t even have a mouth to smile or frown with.

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:39.120
And that makes her more of a blank canvas;
she can be whatever we want her to be.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.060
But many people feel a connection with Gudetama
because of its gloomy personality.

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:49.020
This approach to cuteness extends beyond appearances
— it evokes a reaction.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:53.920
These characters can make you laugh, or feel
relaxed, and you can relate to them by observing

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:55.940
their personality.

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:59.760
So at a time of confusion and turmoil all
around the world —

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.140
maybe this is just what we need:
An egg yolk with a little bum, that’s just

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:05.520
done dealing with life.

